## /etc/qubes/policy.d/30-wgq.policy
##
## Read this file before you copy it into dom0.  Together with the Salt
## formula it is the entire dom0 footprint of this project: no installer,
## no binaries, no scripts.
##
## What these two lines do
## -----------------------
## They let the wgq-mgmt qube read and replace the *outbound firewall rules*
## of any qube tagged `wgq-zone` (the Salt formula applies that tag to each
## sys-wgq-<zone> it creates, and to nothing else).
##
## That firewall is the layer that actually protects you.  It is enforced by
## the VPN qube's net qube, so it holds even if the VPN qube is compromised,
## and it fails closed when the firewall service is not running.  Handing a
## qube the ability to rewrite it is not a small grant, which is why the
## default below is `ask`.
##
## admin.vm.firewall.Set replaces the whole rule list in one call and Qubes
## reloads it automatically, so there is no window in which a partial rule
## set is live.  That is why this is safer than the qvm-firewall command
## sequence it replaces, not merely more convenient.
##
## ask vs allow
## ------------
##   ask    - every change raises a confirmation prompt in dom0 showing the
##            source qube and the target.  You still approve the security
##            boundary; you just stop transcribing IP addresses by hand.
##            This is the default and the recommendation.
##
##   allow  - unattended.  Choose this only if you accept that a compromised
##            wgq-mgmt can then silently rewrite the endpoint allowlist of
##            every VPN qube, i.e. can switch off the protection described
##            above.  wgq-mgmt reaches the network through sys-firewall and
##            runs an HTTP client against your VPN provider, so it is not a
##            qube with no attack surface.
##
## The grant is narrow either way: admin.vm.firewall.* cannot create qubes,
## change a netvm, attach devices, or read anything else.
##
## Installing
## ----------
##   1. Read the two lines below.
##   2. sudo cp 30-wgq.policy /etc/qubes/policy.d/30-wgq.policy
##   3. qvm-tags sys-wgq-work list          # confirm the tag is where you think
##
## To revoke, delete the file.  `wgq firewall` then falls back to printing a
## qvm-firewall command block for you to paste, and nothing else changes.

admin.vm.firewall.Get  *  wgq-mgmt  @tag:wgq-zone  allow  target=dom0
admin.vm.firewall.Set  *  wgq-mgmt  @tag:wgq-zone  ask    target=dom0
